  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nIn last night's discussion, I made a reference to a rather old song \nfrom the 80s. Tonight, we are going even further back, to when I was \na little kid, the 1970s. And this song, and this band, are very \nappropriate for today, at least after any showers end shortly after \nsunset. The song is September, and the band is Earth, Wind and Fire. \nMeaning on earth (at least in western and central Wyoming), the wind \nimpacts today will be from wind and fire, and it is occurring in \nSeptember. Just not on the twenty first night, but on the fourteenth \nday of the month. \n\nThe culprits for this are an upper level low now spinning over \nnorthern Idaho that will move eastward today. Most of the moisture \nshould be gone by shortly after sunrise, with dry weather returning \nfor the entire area after that. The low will be close enough to \nkeep a decent pressure gradient across the area as well. A jet \nstreak will be moving across the northern portions of the state as \nwell. All this adds up to a rather windy day across most of the \narea. Most of western and central Wyoming has at least a 2 out of 3 \nchance of wind gusts over 30 mph today, with a greater than 1 out of \n2 chance of gusts over 45 mph in the windier portions of the area. \nAs for the high wind warning, we will leave it up, but it looks \nborderline. The best chance would be in areas like near Clark and \nto the west of Cody, where there is around a 1 in 2 chance of wind \ngusts over 55 mph. There is still the chance of a mountain wave in \nplaces like Cody and possibly Dubois or even Lander, but not enough \nconfidence to add to the warning. The strongest wind in the north \nshould be over by around noon, but gusty wind will continue through \nthe day before decreasing tonight as the jet streak moves away and \nthe atmosphere decouples. \n\nThe other concern is fire weather. There will certainly be plenty of \nwind across the area, so that is a slam dunk. The area for low \nhumidity also looks solid, covering areas mainly south of the Owl \nCreek and Bridger Range as well as Sweetwater County. If there is a \nmitigating factor, it is that some areas received a decent rain \ntonight, which should wet fuels at least initially. It has been \nspotty though, so we will keep it as is. Elsewhere, relative \nhumidity should remain above critical levels. However, elevated fire \nweather will be likely everywhere as most locations will see \nhumidity under 25 percent this afternoon. As for temperatures, it \nwill be cooler behind the front, with highs fairly close to \nseasonal normals for the middle of September.\n\nTuesday looks like a fairly quiet day for most with sunshine mixing \nwith some clouds. Some increased moisture may try to return to far \nsouthern Wyoming and could bring a stray shower or thunderstorm. \nMost activity should hold off until after sunset though. Moisture \nmay push through the southern third of the area to bring a spotty \nshower or thunderstorm, but most areas will not see one. \n\nThe reasoning for the rest of the week remains similar to the past \ncouple of days. And upper level low will move from the Pacific \nNorthwest to the northeast and eventually to the east. There is a \nbit of more model consensus this morning in taking the low over \nMontana late in the week rather than into Wyoming. However, there \nare still some differences in the exact position as well as the \nspeed in which it moves through. Southwest flow will keep some \nmonsoonal moisture moving into the area, as well as bring a return \nof above normal temperatures. With the increased moisture, there \nwill be a chance of showers and thunderstorms each day from \nWednesday through Friday and possibly into Saturday as well. At this \npoint, Thursday and Friday may be the most active days. However, \nthere is still a ton of model spread regarding the placement of \ntiming of the most numerous showers and storms. As a result, we kept \nlargely broad brushed the POPs. At this point, drier air looks to \nreturn for the second half of the weekend.",
